WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.620
A secure ASP.NET Core Docker image baseline starts by treating images as supply-chain artifacts.

2
00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:15.720
Know what is inside the image, which base image it uses, who can build it, who can publish it, and how the exact production artifact is identified.

3
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:23.120
Use trusted ASP.NET Core runtime images for production and keep SDK images in build stages.

4
00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:32.500
Multi-stage builds should separate restore, build, publish, and runtime concerns so the final image contains only required runtime artifacts.

5
00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:38.780
Keep the build context clean with .dockerignore, narrow copy instructions, and review of final contents.

6
00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:46.800
Keep secrets out of Docker layers, build arguments, environment instructions, logs, caches, and final artifacts.

7
00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:51.940
Use appropriate build-secret mechanisms when restricted access is truly needed.

8
00:00:52.220 --> 00:01:01.740
Run as non-root where practical, with intentional file ownership, writable paths, ports, temporary directories, and runtime assumptions.

9
00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:14.960
Ship only required runtime contents while preserving compatibility for certificates, time zones, globalization, native libraries, and operational diagnostics that the service genuinely needs.

10
00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:18.040
Finally, manage images over time.

11
00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:30.460
Track tags, digests, metadata, SBOMs, provenance, scans, patch status, registry permissions, promotion workflow, rollback plan, and release ownership.

12
00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:37.760
Image security is not a one-time Dockerfile cleanup; it is a repeatable release and operations habit.
